# Dependencies — package-lock.json is committed, so this is reproducible.
node_modules/

# Build output
dist/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Nx local cache and daemon state. nx.json IS committed; this never is.
.nx/

# Secrets. .env.example IS committed; the real thing never is.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# Claude Code: launch.json is shared project config and stays tracked.
# settings.local.json is per-machine permissions and does not.
.claude/settings.local.json

# The vault is WORKING STATE, not a fixture, and none of it is committed.
#
# The seed notes used to be committed here — demo fixtures like the mock
# connectors. They described a programme that no longer exists once the
# connectors read `graph.json`, and more to the point a committed vault means
# running the demo dirties the repo: `seedNotes` writes into it on first boot,
# so every clone that was opened once has a diff.
#
# The claims now ship in `fixtures/notes/`, which IS committed, and are copied
# in on first boot. Input and working state, kept apart — the same split
# `fixtures/events.jsonl` and `vault/raw/events.jsonl` already have.
#
# ANCHORED, and it has to be. `vault/` with no leading slash is a gitignore
# pattern that matches a directory called `vault` at ANY depth — including
# `libs/vault`, the library. Git itself was unaffected, because ignore rules do
# not apply to files already tracked, so nothing looked wrong: `git status` was
# clean and every file was committed. **nx** resolves the pattern against the
# filesystem instead, so `@mc/vault` was not in the project graph at all, and
# `npm run typecheck:all` — the check CLAUDE.md calls authoritative, and the
# only one that applies `types: []` to the platform-neutral libs — silently
# skipped it. The root `tsc -b` still covered it, which is why this never
# surfaced as an error.
/vault/

# The record of the two August planning sessions — four named colleagues,
# verbatim quotes, meeting times and live board ids. It is genuinely useful to
# the team and has no purpose for anybody outside it, and pseudonymising it
# would leave a document whose entire value is who said what, minus the who.
# Kept locally, excluded from publication. DIRECTION.md carries the decisions.
docs/HACKATHON.md

# Editor / OS noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/

# Generated documentation. The markdown files are the sources.
docs/html/

# Live-source working data: fetched payloads, the derived graph, the live vault.
/live-raw/
/live-graph/
/live-vault/
/live-status-map.json

# THE RE-IDENTIFICATION KEY for a sanitised export. It maps every invented name,
# ticket key and channel back to the real one, which makes it the single file
# that would undo the entire export. It is deliberately written OUTSIDE the
# export directory so that copying the export cannot pick it up by accident.
# It never leaves this machine and it is never committed.
/.demo-map.json

# The sanitised export itself. It is *designed* to be safe to copy — prose is
# invented and `export-demo-graph.mts` refuses to finish if a real token
# survives — but it is ignored by default anyway, because the failure mode of
# `git add -A` sweeping up a directory derived from live data is bad enough
# that opting in should be a deliberate edit rather than an omission.
/demo-graph/
/demo-vault/
